 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What is CLN2?",
   "text": "A paper reports that CLN2 disease is caused by changes in a gene called TPP1 [E4eae0df, Ed248e83]. A paper reports that this gene normally makes an enzyme, a small helper protein, called tripeptidyl peptidase 1, and in CLN2 this enzyme does not work enough, which is called a lysosomal enzyme deficiency, where a lysosome is a tiny recycling part inside a cell [E3c230b3, Ec8b6491]. A paper reports that because this enzyme is deficient, a material called ceroid lipofuscin builds up inside cells [Ee036853]. A paper reports that this material builds up inside nerve cells, which are the cells that carry signals in the brain [E04adc14]. A paper reports that this storage is linked with a loss of neurons over time, though exactly how is not fully known [E8ddd2a4]. According to ClinVar, one specific change in the TPP1 gene, called c.509-2A>T, has been found to cause CLN2 [E4ddeac4].",
   "citations": [
    "E4eae0df",
    "Ed248e83",
    "E3c230b3",
    "Ec8b6491",
    "Ee036853",
